summary:
This contents page (printed xiii in the header) covers three chapters.

**Chapter 17, Image generation (printed 508, PDF p.532):**
- 17.1 deep learning for image generation — sampling from latent spaces of images, variational autoencoders, implementing a VAE with Keras;
- 17.2 diffusion models — the Oxford Flowers dataset, a U-Net denoising autoencoder, diffusion time and diffusion schedule, the training process, the generation process, a custom visualization callback, and "It's go time!";
- 17.3 text-to-image models — exploring the latent space of a text-to-image model.

**Chapter 18, Best practices for the real world (printed 538, PDF p.562):**
- 18.1 getting the most out of your models — hyperparameter optimization and model ensembling;
- 18.2 scaling up training with multiple devices — multi-GPU training, distributed training in practice, TPU training;
- 18.3 lower-precision computation — floating-point precision, float16 inference, mixed-precision training, loss scaling, float8 training, and quantization for faster inference.

**Chapter 19, The future of AI (printed 564, PDF p.588):**
- 19.1 the limitations of deep learning — struggling to adapt to novelty, high sensitivity to phrasing and other distractors, difficulty learning generalizable programs, and the risk of anthropomorphizing models;
- 19.2 "Scale isn't all you need" — automatons vs. intelligent agents, local vs. extreme generalization, the purpose of intelligence, and climbing the spectrum of generalization.

The chapter 19 headings make the book's stance visible: the authors argue that scaling current models is not sufficient for general intelligence (the arguments themselves are on the cited pages, not here).
